<!-- cell: title-page id="5476b86d-d1ab-4d5d-a4ea-7d1794951b8e" title="The Night Train" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="2bed29f5-c38c-492e-94d8-ac71af323204" -->

The night train from Prague to Vienna left at 22:14, rocking out of the station into the dark with its blinds half down. Dana Kessler sat in seat 31 of car 7 with the drive taped beneath the insole of her left boot, as she had been taught, and counted the stops she would not get off at.

She had carried things for Anton for six years: documents, money once, a small sealed box she had never asked about. Anton had found her when she had nothing, and she had paid him back with the only thing she had, which was never asking questions.

At 23:02 her phone buzzed. Car 9, compartment 4. Hand it to the man with the newspaper. Then go home. A.

She walked the swaying corridor past carriages that smelled of coffee and wet coats. Through the glass of compartment 4 she saw him: grey coat, folded newspaper, and an expensive steel watch with a red second hand.

She had seen that watch before, in Kraków, on the wrist of a man walking out of a café a minute before her contact was found dead inside it. She had told Anton about the watch. She had told only Anton.

Dana stood very still while the train rocked through the dark and let the knowledge settle. Anton had sold the drive, and he had sold her with it. The man with the newspaper was not there to collect. He was there to finish.

She locked herself in the toilet in car 8. With hands that would not stop shaking, she peeled the drive out of her boot and pushed it deep into the lining of her coat, then wrapped her dead hotel key card from Kraków in the same black tape and slid it into a matchbox.

She knocked on compartment 4. "From Anton," she said, and held out the matchbox. The man smiled without looking at it. His eyes were on her hands. "Sit," he said. "Two minutes," said Dana, steady as a station clock. "Toilet."

The train was slowing for Břeclav. She walked, not ran, to the end of car 9, opened the door onto the cold and stepped down onto the platform as the brakes hissed, and she did not look back until the train had gone.

In the morning she posted the drive to an address in Zurich that Anton did not know existed, bought a new phone, and threw the old one into the river.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="07b6f5a1-78ec-419c-90c7-2cd595b3ecf4" title="Dana Kessler" words="23-57, 58-103, 162-190, 203-218, 246-299, 300-313, 330-339, 346-382, 383-412" -->

A courier who has run errands for Anton for six years without a question, out of gratitude for being rescued. Disciplined and frightened in equal measure, she wants only to do the job and go home, and survives by keeping her nerve.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="14275b87-287d-48ba-ab67-b003bf28e601" title="Anton" words="58-103, 104-124, 191-202, 219-245, 300-313, 383-412" -->

Dana's handler, who took her in when she had nothing and has lived off her loyalty ever since; he has sold the drive, and sold her with it.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="2003b0ba-70ae-4778-af30-1badba9264cf" title="The night train from Prague to Vienna" words="0-22, 125-161, 203-218, 246-254, 340-382" -->

The swaying night train — its corridors and compartments, the toilet in car 8, the slowing at Břeclav — where the handover and the escape play out.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="d57caffe-7e87-4c6b-84ed-372fa6287814" title="Betrayal" words="139-161, 162-202, 219-245, 280-299, 300-329, 383-412" -->

Anton's betrayal of Dana, read in a stranger's watch that only Anton knew she had seen, and her answer to it: the decoy, the vanished drive, the old phone in the river.

<!-- cell: plot id="8004f84f-1664-40d7-8f44-3289df7d42d1" title="The handover" nodes="n1:0-57, n2:104-124, n3:139-161, n4:162-202, n5:219-245, n6:255-299, n7:300-313, n8:340-382, n9:383-412" -->

Told to hand the drive to a man on the train, Dana recognises his watch from a killing in Kraków, realises Anton has betrayed her, hands over a decoy and leaves the train at Břeclav with the real drive.
